<title>JAPAN: Japan says won&quot;t limit Austr. imports over anthrax.</title>
<headline>Japan says won&quot;t limit Austr. imports over anthrax.</headline>
<dateline>TOKYO 1997-02-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>Japan is carefully watching the outbreak of anthrax in dairy herds in Australia, but has no immediate plans to limit beef or dairy imports from that country, an Agriculture Ministry official said on Thursday.</p>
<p>&quot;We are aware of the reports, and asked Australia to keep us fully informed,&quot; a ministry official said.</p>
<p>Australian dairy industry and government officials said earlier that 68 animals were killed on about 31 farms in the state of Victoria because of the disease.</p>
<p>Anthrax, a bacterial infection, causes rapid death in cattle, sheep and goats. It rarely affects humans but can be contracted through a small cut or scratch.</p>
<p>&quot;We&quot;ve received several queries from local trade houses on what action the Japanese government will take,&quot; the ministry official said. &quot;But we have no immediate plan to limit imports from Australia,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>-- Tokyo Commodities desk +81-3-3432-7391</p>
